1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,416
Case studies, part one: liver cancer.

2
00:00:03,784 --> 00:00:10,015
We use RMTI as a triage layer for
hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance.

3
00:00:10,317 --> 00:00:14,179
The question is: which patient
groups need surveillance first,

4
00:00:14,309 --> 00:00:16,892
and which lever moves each group the most?

5
00:00:17,271 --> 00:00:23,848
A reminder before we start. RMTI is
a research prototype: not validated,

6
00:00:23,988 --> 00:00:28,986
not a medical device, and not for
patient-care or emergency decisions.

7
00:00:29,317 --> 00:00:34,195
Everything here uses illustrative values,
published rows, or aggregate counts.

8
00:00:34,458 --> 00:00:38,961
No patient data appears. Here is
how the inputs map to the liver.

9
00:00:39,288 --> 00:00:41,780
L comes from a validated risk score.

10
00:00:42,084 --> 00:00:46,195
E is the share of the at-risk population
in the surveillance footprint.

11
00:00:46,483 --> 00:00:49,746
V is liver reserve, from the ALBI grade.

12
00:00:50,022 --> 00:00:56,261
And rho is surveillance effectiveness, on
a published ladder from none, at 0.15,

13
00:00:56,445 --> 00:00:59,138
to optimised, at 0.65.

14
00:00:59,468 --> 00:01:04,148
Four published cohort rows
from the RMTI HCC calculator:

15
00:01:04,439 --> 00:01:10,080
untreated hepatitis B, active viral
cirrhosis, alcohol-related cirrhosis,

16
00:01:10,215 --> 00:01:12,695
and cured hepatitis C cirrhosis.

17
00:01:13,009 --> 00:01:17,298
The after values for resilience are
back-solved to match the published table,

18
00:01:17,531 --> 00:01:20,298
so they are scenarios, not observations.

19
00:01:20,679 --> 00:01:25,656
Each group travels the cube:
likelihood, exposure, vulnerability.

20
00:01:26,038 --> 00:01:30,215
Notice how far out untreated
hepatitis B sits on likelihood.

21
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:36,147
With today’s surveillance, untreated
hepatitis B is the only group in tier 3, high,

22
00:01:36,377 --> 00:01:42,308
with R of 0.31. The three cirrhosis
groups sit in tier 2, moderate.

23
00:01:42,568 --> 00:01:47,520
After the improvement package, cured
hepatitis C falls to tier 1, low.

24
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:51,773
But untreated hepatitis
B stays in tier 3, high,

25
00:01:52,039 --> 00:01:54,966
even though its R falls by 39 percent.

26
00:01:55,429 --> 00:02:01,165
Lever one: resilience. Walk untreated
hepatitis B up the surveillance ladder.

27
00:02:01,441 --> 00:02:06,438
None, usual care, outreach,
specialist: R falls,

28
00:02:06,438 --> 00:02:10,585
but stays in tier 3. Only
optimised surveillance,

29
00:02:10,585 --> 00:02:15,475
ultrasound plus AFP with reliable
access to cure, at 0.65,

30
00:02:15,656 --> 00:02:21,840
brings R to 0.12, below 0.15, into tier 2.

31
00:02:22,221 --> 00:02:25,240
Which single lever would
be enough to leave tier 3?

32
00:02:25,608 --> 00:02:31,376
Resilience needs to reach at least
0.57, which the optimised rung exceeds.

33
00:02:31,682 --> 00:02:35,202
Vulnerability would have to fall to 0.26,

34
00:02:35,410 --> 00:02:40,999
below the lowest ALBI anchor of
0.35, so reserve alone cannot do it.

35
00:02:41,303 --> 00:02:47,050
Exposure would need to fall to
0.34, or likelihood to 0.43.

36
00:02:47,577 --> 00:02:51,359
Lever two: treat the cause.
In the published table,

37
00:02:51,557 --> 00:02:56,206
untreated hepatitis B
carries a likelihood of 0.90,

38
00:02:56,470 --> 00:02:59,872
and suppressed hepatitis B 0.20.

39
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:04,441
Move only that lever, holding the
other inputs of the untreated group,

40
00:03:04,616 --> 00:03:09,157
and R falls from 0.31 to 0.07,

41
00:03:09,376 --> 00:03:14,506
from tier 3 to tier 2.
Lever three: liver reserve.

42
00:03:14,776 --> 00:03:19,786
In the framework, fibrosis progression
and decompensation raise vulnerability.

43
00:03:20,036 --> 00:03:24,858
Using the ALBI anchors as a what-if
on the active viral cirrhosis group:

44
00:03:25,349 --> 00:03:30,131
at grade one, R is 0.067; at grade two,

45
00:03:30,266 --> 00:03:35,338
0.115; at grade three, 0.164,

46
00:03:35,512 --> 00:03:40,435
crossing into tier 3. This is a
what-if sweep, not patient data.

47
00:03:40,738 --> 00:03:46,256
To ground V in real measurements,
we use the Faria et al.

48
00:03:46,432 --> 00:03:49,812
cohort from Harvard Dataverse. From
measured albumin and bilirubin,

49
00:03:49,812 --> 00:03:52,703
each person has an ALBI grade,
and we use only the group counts,

50
00:03:52,703 --> 00:03:54,571
never patient-level records.

51
00:03:54,864 --> 00:03:58,290
Weighting the grade anchors by
those counts gives vulnerability:

52
00:03:58,489 --> 00:04:03,798
0.52 for the cancer cohort
and 0.48 for cirrhosis.

53
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:06,559
A real measurement, feeding one lever.

54
00:04:06,869 --> 00:04:12,244
Putting the levers side by side for untreated
hepatitis B, each moved by ten points,

55
00:04:12,244 --> 00:04:18,175
from largest to smallest: vulnerability,
exposure, likelihood and resilience.

56
00:04:18,502 --> 00:04:22,138
R falls by 0.057 at most.

57
00:04:22,401 --> 00:04:28,010
The ranking depends on the group,
which is why RMTI is run per cohort.

58
00:04:28,287 --> 00:04:33,996
What this shows and does not show. The
arithmetic and the levers are RMTI’s own,

59
00:04:33,996 --> 00:04:35,996
applied to published rows.

60
00:04:36,242 --> 00:04:39,781
But fibrosis-stage inputs are
not in the current calculators,

61
00:04:39,919 --> 00:04:43,795
so ALBI anchors stand in, the
after values are scenarios,

62
00:04:43,938 --> 00:04:46,965
and nothing here is validated
against incident cancer.

63
00:04:47,167 --> 00:04:51,218
Surveillance decisions rest
with clinicians and guidelines.

64
00:04:51,502 --> 00:04:56,874
That is RMTI on the liver: one
equation, three levers you can pull,

65
00:04:56,874 --> 00:04:58,188
and a clear view of what each would take.

66
00:04:58,277 --> 00:05:03,544
I’m Dr. Cesar Marolla. The
code is open under Apache 2.0,

67
00:05:03,813 --> 00:05:08,016
the demo uses no patient data, and
nothing here is medical advice.

